reference:
The Time\Duration class Time\Duration
&reftitle.intro;
Represents a fixed length of elapsed time, such as a timeout or the time measured by a stopwatch, with nanosecond precision. A duration consists of a non-negative number of whole Time\Duration::$seconds, a number of Time\Duration::$nanoseconds below one second, and a sign stored in Time\Duration::$negative.
Unlike DateInterval, a Time\Duration does not have calendar units such as days, months or years, because those do not map to a fixed number of seconds: a day can be 23 or 25 hours long across a DST transition, and months have different lengths. The largest unit a duration can be created from is therefore the hour. Use DateInterval for calendar arithmetic on DateTimeImmutable objects, and Time\Duration for unambiguous amounts of time, for example the timeout of the I/O polling API.
Objects of this class are immutable. They are created with one of the Time\Duration::from*() factory methods, and every operation such as Time\Duration::add or Time\Duration::multiplyBy returns a new object instead of modifying the existing one. A duration can be negative, which is typically the result of Time\Duration::sub or Time\Duration::negate. A duration of zero is never negative.
Two durations can be compared with the comparison operators (<, <=, ==, !=, >=, > and <=>) as well as with Time\Duration::compare. The arithmetic operators are not supported; use the methods of this class instead.
The maximum length of a duration is 9223372035 seconds, roughly 292 years, on 64-bit platforms, and 2147483647 seconds, roughly 68 years, on 32-bit platforms. Any operation whose result would exceed this range throws a Time\TimeException.
&reftitle.classsynopsis;
Time
